  "example_explanation": "In this example, the pointer 'p' is of type 'Base*' but points to a 'Derived' object. When 'p->foo()' is called, the program uses the vtable pointer stored within the 'Derived' object to locate the correct 'foo' function implementation. The compiler generates a vtable for 'Base' and 'Derived' classes, with 'Derived' overriding the 'foo' entry. At runtime, the vtable pointer in the object points to 'Derived's vtable, ensuring the 'Derived' version of 'foo' is invoked. This demonstrates how the vtable mechanism enables dynamic dispatch based on the actual object type, a core aspect of C++ object model internals.",
